<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Malbork Castle</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Marienburg, Castle of the Teutonic Order in Malbork</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malbork">Malbork</a> Castle, German Marienburg, is a vast brick fortress on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nogat">Nogat</a> river in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, built by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> from the late 13th century. From 1309 it was the seat of the Grand Master and capital of the Order's state in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>. It withstood a Polish siege after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-grunwald">Battle of Grunwald</a> in 1410, but passed to Poland in 1457 and is now a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> World Heritage site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/malbork-castle.jpg" alt="Malbork Castle" width="320" height="203" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gregy · CC BY-SA 3.0 pl · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zesp%C3%B3%C5%82_Zamku_Krzy%C5%BCackiego_MALBORK_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> had been founded in 1191 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a>, and after that stronghold fell to the Muslims in 1291 it moved its headquarters to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> before arriving in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>. The castle was built after the conquest of Old Prussia, and its main purpose was to strengthen the Order's control after it suppressed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-prussian-uprising">Great Prussian Uprising</a> of the Baltic tribes in 1274. Work lasted until around 1300 under Commander Heinrich von Wilnowe, and it was named Marienburg for Mary, patron saint of the Order. The castle stands on the south-eastern bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nogat">Nogat</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The castle grew in importance after the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gdansk">Gdańsk</a> and Eastern Pomerania in 1308, when the Order's administrative centre moved there from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elblag">Elbing</a>. Grand Master Siegfried von Feuchtwangen, who came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, undertook the next phase of building and in 1309 relocated his headquarters to Marienburg. The complex has three separate castles, the High, Middle and Lower, divided by dry moats and towers, on a site of nearly 21 hectares, and once housed about 3,000 brothers in arms. The Order collected river tolls, held a monopoly on the amber trade, and the town's membership in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a> brought Hanseatic meetings there. Most historians accept the years from 1274 to 1406 as the construction period.</p><p>After the defeat at Grunwald in the summer of 1410, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-von-plauen">Heinrich von Plauen</a> led the defence in a siege during which the town outside was razed. By 1456, in the Thirteen Years' War, the Order was in severe financial distress. Its Bohemian mercenaries, long unpaid, agreed to sell the castle, and in 1457 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casimir-iv-jagiellon">Casimir IV Jagiellon</a> entered it in triumph. For over 300 years the castle served as a Polish royal residence, home to the Polish Admiralty from 1568, a royal mint from 1584 and the Commonwealth's largest arsenal. Swedish forces occupied it in 1626 and 1629 and again from 1656 to 1660.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-partition-of-poland">First Partition of Poland</a> in 1772 the castle became a Prussian barracks and poorhouse, and in 1794 the architect David Gilly surveyed it. His son Friedrich Gilly's engravings, published from 1799 to 1803, led the Prussian public to rediscover it, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a> it became a symbol of Prussian history. Restoration began in 1816 under Theodor von Schön. In the early 1930s the Nazis used it as a pilgrimage destination, and fighting with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> in early 1945 destroyed more than half of it. Restoration has been ongoing since 1962, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> named it a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> in December 1997.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Malbork Castle” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1308 the knights seized Danzig and Pomerelia, and in 1309 the Grand Master moved his seat from Venice to <b>Marienburg</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-the-teutonic-order">State of the Teutonic Order · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Family members served as knights and administrators, and Heinrich von Plauen defended <b>Marienburg</b> after the Battle of Grunwald and became Grand Master of the State of the Teutonic Order.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-plauen">Lordship of Plauen</a></li></ul></section>
